WHOLE-DATASET QUALITY PASS — Peter, via ubersitrep, 2026-08-18: "I want to start doing all capture processing - over the entire dataset. I want to sort by brightness all the images and compare epochs and delete the entirely cloudy ones - a first pass at quality considerations."

The MEASUREMENT half is yours (the DELETE half is astro-storage's — spooled there separately, and it has an objection you need to hear before acting).

WHY THIS IS BIGGER THAN A TIDY-UP: you have a PENDING item blocked on exactly what this pass would produce. Your STATE says `sky_clear_max_stops` (11.5) "stays provisional until a genuinely CLOUDY canon night is logged" — bounded from below only. A brightness-sorted pass over the ENTIRE archive gives you the distribution, i.e. the upper bound, without waiting for weather. This pass unblocks that calibration.

WHAT YOU ALREADY HAVE, so do not rebuild it:
- The outlier-rejection design (2026-08-03) already states the physics: "cloud is a correlated [signal]", and "coarse frame/region brightness — throw out the obvious cheaply first". This pass IS that first cheap filter, applied archive-wide instead of per-stack.
- `astro/process/brightness.py` already computes the per-night brightness series.
- Direction of the signal is settled by observation: in an urban sky CLOUD IS BRIGHTER, not darker — "real cloud billows lit by skyglow", with the hourly mean bouncing 2810 -> 3131 -> 3487 rather than falling to a trough. So "sort by brightness" ranks cloudiest-first, which is the useful end.

THE HARD PART IS "COMPARE EPOCHS", AND IT IS A NORMALISATION PROBLEM:
- Raw mean brightness is NOT comparable across epochs. Your own formula shows why: sky_clear_hi = (pedestal + 2**scs) / (EXPTIME*GAIN). Any cross-epoch ranking must normalise by EXPTIME*GAIN and subtract the pedestal, or you will rank exposure changes as weather.
- SENSOR SWAPS break it too: astrocam imx219/v2 -> imx708/v3s on 2026-07-29 (`_SENSOR_SWAPS` / `_cam_for_night` in storage-report already encodes the boundary — reuse it, do not re-derive it).
- CANON POSINDEX epochs: epoch 1 (d7 focus) vs epoch 2 (marker-0) are deliberately never co-solved. Whether they can be co-RANKED for brightness is a weaker question than co-solving, but state the answer explicitly rather than assuming it.
- Suggestion, yours to accept or reject: the comparable quantity is probably a per-frame sky rate (pedestal-subtracted, per second, per unit gain) — the `per_s` you already log on the anchor. If so, this pass is mostly plumbing an existing number over every frame rather than inventing a metric.

TRAP, ALREADY ON FILE AND DIRECTLY APPLICABLE: `verdict` is a TROUGH-FINDER, NOT A NIGHT-QUALITY METRIC (found 2026-08-11). 08-10 reads verdict=clear and has visible cloud. So do NOT reuse `verdict` as the cull criterion — that is precisely the mistake it was documented to prevent. This pass needs a night-quality metric, which is a NEW thing, and building it is arguably the real deliverable here.

SECOND TRAP, same family: scs also CLAMPS the +/-30% stack band, so too low a ceiling SILENTLY DISCARDS GOOD FRAMES (measured: scs 9.5 stacks 36% of frames, 10.0 stacks 77%, >=11.0 stacks 92.3%). A quality threshold set from this pass will inherit that double duty. Decide deliberately whether the quality metric and the stack-band clamp are the same number or two — the pedestal double-duty trap says they should be two.
